The technical explanation is the the AoY isn't an "artifact" in the
same sense as, say, the quest artifacts are.  I'm pretty sure you can
#name the book, bell and candelabrum anything you want, too.

Anyway, I did think of a way to make fake amulets of Yendor a little
bit more interesting some time ago: make any newly generated fake
amulets have the same #name as the real amulet currently has.

So, yes, you can still name the real AoY "REAL", but the next time
Rodney steals it and double-troubles, the fake the clone is carrying
will _also_ be named "REAL".  Of course, that still leaves several
easy ways to tell the real and fake amulets apart, but at least you'd
have to spend a _little_ effort on it.

Any patch writers or fork maintainers are welcome to steal this idea
and run with it. :)


> The Heart of Ahriman is an artifact luckstone (barb quest prize) and a
> unique item, so you can't make another one by #naming an ordinary
> luckstone.  The slip is the tell - you can have as many flintstones
> named The Heart of Ahriman as you want though.

The funny thing is, as far as I've looked at the source, the game
should actually be able to handle e.g. non-artifact luckstones named
"The Heart of Ahriman" just fine; it's only the code in do_oname() and
related functions that complains if you try to make one.  It seems to
me that just a few simple changes to do_name.c and artifact.c would
allow the naming restrictions for non-artifacts to be removed entirely
(except of course for the names of the two artifacts that can actually
be created by #naming).

I suppose it's possible that there might be some other obscure piece
of code that would also need changing.  But in general, at least
modern versions of nethack don't rely on object names to tell what's
an artifact and what isn't; they use the obj->oartifact field instead.

